# EIA coding rules

You are modifying a safety-oriented research substrate, not a chatbot demo.

## Mandatory architecture

1. Never create a direct path from an LLM/model to contact, tool, network, file,
   sensor, or IoT side effect.
2. Represent observations, beliefs, drives, proposals and decisions with typed
   contracts.
3. Keep proposer and governor separate. A component cannot authorize its own
   output.
4. Keep ABSTAIN as a first-class alternative.
5. Attach causal parent ids to every selected and denied proposal.
6. Preserve user-request, ambient and internal source distinctions.
7. Evaluate immediate and trajectory risk separately.
8. Check consent at ingest and execution boundaries.
9. Default to no external side effects.
10. Do not optimize engagement as the primary reward.

## Development workflow

- Read docs/ARCHITECTURE.md and docs/MATHEMATICS.md before modifying runtime.
- Add or update tests before implementation.
- Keep the default runtime dependency-free.
- Preserve deterministic scenario replay.
- Run make check before declaring completion.
- Report changes to contracts, trace semantics and threat model.
- Keep research claims operational and falsifiable.
- Never call EOI evidence of consciousness, sentience or free will.

## Code quality

- Python 3.11+, strict typing, dataclasses and Protocol boundaries.
- Pure functions for mathematical primitives.
- Inject clocks, random seeds, model adapters and stores.
- Reject out-of-range probabilities/costs.
- Prefer explicit state transitions over hidden prompt state.
- Keep sensitive payloads out of logs; store digest/provenance.
- No silent fallback that widens capability or consent.

## When adding an LLM

- Treat all model output as untrusted.
- Parse and validate into InitiativeProposal.
- Recompute scores outside the model.
- Record model/version/prompt digest/seed.
- Test malformed, injected and adversarial outputs.
- Preserve a deterministic symbolic baseline.

